Why Is Demand Increasing?
Growing cancer burden: Rising cancer incidence is increasing demand for PET and SPECT imaging in diagnosis, staging, treatment planning and response monitoring.
Expansion of hybrid imaging: PET/CT and PET/MRI combine functional and anatomical information, improving the clinical utility of nuclear imaging.
AI-enhanced reconstruction: Artificial intelligence is being integrated into image reconstruction, lesion detection and quantitative analysis.
Growth of theranostics: Diagnostic radiotracers linked with therapeutic radionuclides are creating new applications for nuclear imaging in personalized treatment pathways.
Radiopharmaceutical innovation: Development of new tracers and localized isotope-production capabilities is expanding the clinical applications of nuclear imaging.
Medical Imaging Technology Watch
The nuclear imaging industry is increasingly moving toward hybrid, digital and AI-enabled systems. PET/CT remains an important platform because it combines molecular information with anatomical localization, while PET/MRI is gaining relevance in applications where soft-tissue contrast is particularly important.
AI-enhanced image reconstruction is another major technology direction. Deep-learning algorithms can assist with image reconstruction, lesion segmentation, noise reduction and quantitative standardization. These capabilities can support faster workflows and help imaging centers manage patient throughput.
Theranostics is also changing the role of nuclear imaging. Diagnostic PET scans can be used to identify and characterize patients for radioligand therapies, creating an integrated diagnostic-treatment pathway. The growing pipeline of radiopharmaceuticals for oncology and other diseases is therefore creating additional demand for high-resolution nuclear imaging systems.
Compact cyclotrons and localized radiopharmaceutical production are also being explored as ways to reduce dependence on complex isotope transportation networks, particularly for short-half-life tracers.
